{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00694 - MONDO_18433",
  "verbalization": "Daunorubicin is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) with specific chromosomal translocations, particularly T(6;9)(p23;q34). This relationship is supported by its association with Glasdegib, a drug that targets the same molecular pathway in AML. The evidence suggests that Daunorubicin may be repurposed for this specific subtype of AML, as it is indicated in similar conditions involving T(9;11) and T(6;9) translocations. The shared use of Glasdegib in these contexts indicates a potential mechanistic overlap, though the exact role of Daunorubicin remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of a consistent pathway involving Glasdegib across different AML subtypes suggests that the therapeutic relationship may be driven by a common molecular mechanism, such as the inhibition of certain enzymes or pathways involved in leukemogenesis. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Daunorubicin could be a viable candidate for repurposing in this specific AML subset, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted.",
